    "turn_idx": 80,
    "turn_text": "that video is horrific. It can't happen, period. I think, obviously, everybody knows that, but stuff like that, it just can't happen. Now, I'm not a member of Congress who was sitting there and be like, OK, now let's go pass some law so that it never happens again, because the history of Congress is we run around saying we're going to make sure this never happens again. We pass some law It actually has worse repercussions for what's happening on the issue. And while we took our victory lap, we're on to the next thing. And we don't go back to actually figure out if what we did made sense or is actually working. So I think from a policy perspective, you know, there were bills on policing. The Tim Scott bill, the justice act is one that comes to mind because what that bill actually would have done was help with training and so on and so forth and funding for body cams after what happened with George Floyd. The Democrats in the Senate filibustered the bill. They said it didn't go far enough. They wanted to end qualified immunity, which would have eviscerated police departments. So the one thing I've kind of always seen through this piece is law enforcement starts at home. It's your chief of police. It's your sheriff. It's the training systems that they put in place. It's the recruitment efforts that they put in place. And so what's happened in law enforcement, especially the last couple of years, any sheriff or police chief will tell you, they have had the hardest time recruiting people into the into the world of being a police officer. Because, I mean, look, people are dumb. They see how police are being vilified on TV. Do they want to sign up for that and put not just their lives on the line, which police officers do, but their fiscal health on the line, their families on the line, in case something goes wrong. Most people don't want to do that. So recruitment is down in law enforcement. That's why when these issues come up, we have to be very careful how we talk about them. We can't sensationalize them and politicize them. It has to be about the facts, because when you're off that issue, man, those officers, they got families, they got friends, they're white, they're black, they're Hispanic, a lot of them serve our communities very, very well. So if you're gonna talk about policy, the first policy starts at home. What's going on with recruitment, what's going on with training, and that's the key things that I think you have to figure out a way to address. What we found out in Memphis, is that after all the things that happened with George Floyd and all the protests, the riots, the condemning of police, etc., they have actually ratcheted down their benchmarks for what it takes to become a police officer. So the levels have decreased in terms of the qualifications. That's not good for communities. This is worthy of a",
